metadata.description:
{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One row, up in the toolbars: time signatures, pre-roll, and splitting a long bar in two.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two click levels and the accent pattern, in the docker.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Intelligent track grouping with buttons. Select tracks, tick what should move together (solo, mute, record arm, volume, editing - pan off by default), press GROUP. Every existing group is listed with its name, its members and one tick per behaviour you can flip live; Select / Ungroup per group; and the CLUTCH (REAPER's "all grouping enabled") as a big switch. Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository (right-click the repository in ReaPack > Install All).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Time signatures and their groupings, without touching the tempo.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Colour and name your tracks from what they are: your own ordered rules (import/export SWS Auto Color), instrument families with colours, palettes, gradients and nudges, name-from-items, numbering, and a LIVE mode that re-colours as tracks are added or renamed. Markers and regions too. Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository (right-click the repository in ReaPack > Install All).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Live patch switching for REAPER. Reads a setlist built by StageRig Build, shows a stage view with NOW and NEXT, and switches patches without cutting off what is still ringing. Bind "StageRig Next" / "StageRig Panic" to a footswitch for hands-free use. Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository (right-click the repository in ReaPack > Install All).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Print stems four ways (raw / to the bus / through master / fully wet) and put the session back exactly as it was. Optionally builds a plugin-free mirror tab with the printed files on it, ready to hand to anyone on any DAW. Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository (right-click the repository in ReaPack > Install All).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pro-Tools style "Import Session Data" between open project tabs. Copies FX chains, volume, pan, sends, colour and input FX from the tracks of one project onto the same-named tracks of one or more other tabs, or builds the tracks that are missing. Preview first; one undo point per target project. Automation is never copied. Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository and the SWS extension. ReaPack has no automatic dependencies, so install the libraries too - or right-click the repository in ReaPack > Install All.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Duplicate the selected track EMPTY and armed, ready to record the next layer; the original is disarmed, every other track keeps its arm state. The copy takes the next number: Verse 1 -> Verse 2, V1 -> V2, O1 -> O2 (skipping numbers already used; "Verse" -> "Verse 2"). Needs SWS.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Puts the selected tracks into one track group for EDITING ONLY: media and razor edits on one track happen on all of them (split, trim, move, delete), while faders, mutes, solos and arms stay independent. Press it again on the same tracks to take them OUT of their edit group. The clutch: "Track: Toggle all track grouping enabled" (Jason: Cmd+Shift+G) bypasses every group while you do fine edits; press again to re-engage.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Runs quietly in the background. Click a track's record-arm button: every other track is disarmed. Hold Option while clicking: the track is added and nothing else changes. Arming several tracks in one click (a selection) keeps all of them. Solo is left alone. Run the action again to switch it off; its menu/toolbar state shows on while it runs. Never touches inputs, monitoring, levels or the project file. Option is read by the small helper Scripts/Nathaniel Tools/bin/nt_mods (source: tools/nt_mods.swift); without it the script still works, just always exclusive.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Float the first FX of the selected tracks, or close it if it is already open. Needs SWS.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Open every FX window on the selected (or last touched) track, or close them all if any are open.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Replace whatever is on the selected tracks with the clipboard: delete their items, paste, strip envelopes, save silently. Needs SWS.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fixes the "next submix got swallowed" problem in one key press. Select the track that SHOULD be the last one in its folder and run this: the folder closes right after it, the next track or submix pops out one level, and everything further down keeps its exact shape (the track that used to close the folder closes one level less). Press again to pop the next track out one more level. Cmd+Z undoes it. A folder track itself is ignored; select its last child instead.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposite of Folder End Here. Select the last track of a folder and run this: the very next track (or the whole next submix, with all its tracks) moves inside that folder, and the folder now ends after it. Nothing else changes shape. Cmd+Z undoes it.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Build, dissolve, move and isolate track folders without dragging. Indent / outdent ticked tracks, take a track out of its folder, move a whole folder up or down, auto-group tracks by name, and repair a session whose folder nesting has gone wrong. Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository (right-click the repository in ReaPack > Install All).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Select the stems and busses you want to deliver, then run this once. It:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tab Checks that the selected tracks are exactly the ones with music on them (nothing audible left out,\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 nothing empty or muted selected) and that every track name is unique.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tab Makes "JZ Horns" in the project folder with Trumpet Stems, Trombone Stems, Submixes and\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ALL Trumpets & Trombones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tab Renders your selection as stems plus the full horn mix, with the EFX folder muted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tab Renders only the EFX folder, unmuted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tab Puts your selection, mutes and render settings back exactly as they were.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 6.\tab Files every WAV: trumpet tracks -> Trumpet Stems, trombone tracks -> Trombone Stems,\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 busses/folders -> Submixes, the full mix and the EFX -> ALL Trumpets & Trombones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 7.\tab Quality check: a file for every track, nothing silent, nothing clipping, right length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Report saved in JZ Horns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 8.\tab Offers to copy the whole project folder into the Dropbox folder you paste, and opens it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Never deletes anything: an older WAV with the same name moves to JZ Horns/Previous Renders, and a Dropbox file that would be replaced is kept in "_Replaced by JZ copy". Uses your own render format; does not touch the render queue.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Select two (or more) tracks and press: a NEW folder track appears above them, the selected tracks go inside it, nothing else in the session changes shape. The folder is named from what the children have in common ("Kick In"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 \u8226 ?\tab "Kick Out" -> KICK; "Electric L/R/C" -> ELECTRIC), coloured like its\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 children, and made a little taller so it reads as a folder. Non-adjacent selections are first gathered together under the first one. Needs the Shared Libraries package (nt_hierarchy).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Export many MIDI items or regions as separate .mid files in one go, named from a pattern ($num, $name, $track, $region, $project, $bpm). Every file carries the tempo and time signature at its own start, at the item's own tick resolution, so it opens at the right speed anywhere. Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository (right-click the repository in ReaPack > Install All).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Press once: the selected MIDI items (or the one under the mouse when nothing is selected) open their notes right inside the item in the arrange view, and the track grows tall enough to read and drag single notes. Press again: the notes fold away and the track goes back to the height it had. Made for quick fixes - a wrong note, a late note - without opening the big MIDI editor window. Also set as the double-click on items: double-clicking a MIDI item opens its notes in place; double-clicking an audio item does what it always did.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Export the project MIDI for the time selection (or the whole project if there is none) with one key.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Drop a marker on the nearest bar line to the cursor (or the play position while playing). Never in between beats, never a duplicate.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bind to absolute MIDI CC from a control surface: one knob pans every record-armed track at once, centre-detented, without changing what is selected. Written for the "12 Step Bridge" knob layout.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Runs quietly in the background (start it from __startup.lua or the Actions list). Every project that comes to the front for the first time - REAPER launch, File > New, a new tab, or one opened from disk - gets transport repeat (loop) switched OFF, the mixer shown AND pulled to the front of its docker, and "trim content behind items" ON with auto-crossfade OFF (projects save their own copy of that). New, never-saved projects also get snapping ON and relative grid snap ON. (Saved projects keep their own snap settings.)\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Opens Palette & Look, Folders & Flow, Track Settings Transfer, Stem Print & Handoff, MIDI Batch Export, Group Deck and the Click Strip in REAPER's docker, as tabs, in one go. Run it once from the Actions list, or put it in your startup so the tools are there "at the word go". Apps that are already open are left alone. StageRig is deliberately not included - it is a stage tool, not a studio one.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Arm ONLY the selected tracks (everything else disarmed); press again to disarm all. Never touches pan, unlike the custom action it replaced.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Runs quietly in the background. A horn or voice in the room drowns its own headphone feed, so while you record it sounds soft. While REAPER is recording, every armed track that records its input is turned up (6 dB by default) so you hear yourself properly. The moment recording stops, the fader goes back exactly where it was. The recorded file is not affected: those tracks record the input, before the fader. Move the fader during a take and that move is kept (minus the boost) when recording stops. Change the amount: Extended state "NathanielTools" / "RecordMonitorBoostDB" (0 switches the boost off). Run the action again to switch it off; its menu/toolbar state shows on while it runs.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Jump to the next region and make it the time selection. While playing, playback follows the jump (with smooth seek, on the bar).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Jump to the previous region and make it the time selection. While playing, playback follows the jump (with smooth seek, on the bar).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Runs quietly in the background, next to Duplicate Track. Duplicate Track marks every copy it makes with the name it came from ("Verse"). Rename that copy to something new ("Off Beat 1") and it gets a fresh colour, picked to stand apart from every colour already in the project. Renaming within the family (Verse 2 -> Verse 4) keeps the colour. Each copy is recoloured once; after that the colour is yours. Run the action again to switch it off.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Renders used to start with a burst of stray audio: reverb/delay tails and plugin buffers left over from wherever playback last was. Measured on the studio Mac: after playing a section and rendering the top of the song, the first half-second of the file carried a -32 dBFS tail that was not in the song - even with REAPER's "delay render start" on. Jason's hand workaround was "play 5 s of silence at the end of the project, then render". This script does exactly that, automatically:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tab stops, jumps past the end of the project, plays 4 s of silence through\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 every plugin so every tail decays, stops, puts the cursor back\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa60 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tab loads the "Jason Zac Default" render preset (format, sample rate,\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 regions, file name) into the project, then opens File > Render. Press Render as usual. A countdown appears at the mouse the moment the key is pressed, so the 4 s of silence never looks like "nothing happened". Bound to your render keys (F14, Cmd+Opt+R).\par}
}

version.changelog:
1.3.0 - loads the "Jason Zac Default" render preset every time, so Render always starts from it.
1.2.0 - shows a countdown at the mouse straight away (the silent 4 s looked like the key did nothing);
refuses to run while recording; pressing it twice cancels cleanly and puts the cursor back;
writes one line per press to render-safe.log in the REAPER folder.
1.1.0 - flushes by playing silence past the end (the thing that really clears the tails);
no longer forces "delay render start" (that pops a 30 s dialog on one-click renders).
1.0.0 - first version.

metadata.description:
{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The shared code the rest of the Nathaniel Tools is built on: pointer safety and stable track identity (nt_safe), folder-nesting arithmetic (nt_hierarchy), and ReaImGui version compatibility (nt_imgui).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Solo-in-place the selected tracks (or the tracks of the selected items); press again on the same set to clear every solo. Never moves the cursor unless you turn AUDITION on.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Requires the "Shared Libraries" package from this same repository. ReaPack has no automatic dependencies, so install that one too - or just right-click the repository in ReaPack and choose Install All. Builds a live concert project from a rig spec generated out of a MainStage .concert file. Creates one folder per patch, one track per channel strip, inserts the substitute instrument where one is known, and puts the note tracker in front of every instrument so patch switching can be clean.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tells the running StageRig window to switch to the NEXT patch. Bind this to a footswitch or MIDI pedal (Actions > find "StageRig Next" > Add shortcut > press the pedal) and you never touch the mouse on stage. Does nothing if StageRig is not open.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tells the running StageRig window to PANIC: mute and flush every patch except the one you are playing. Bind it to a second pedal. Does nothing if StageRig is not open.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Adds a stretch marker exactly under the mouse pointer, in the item you are pointing at, without moving the edit cursor. Meant to be assigned as a mouse modifier: Preferences > Mouse modifiers > "Media item bottom half" > left click > Action list > this script. (Nathaniel Tools sets Control+click.) Needs SWS (for the mouse-position lookup).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Insert or edit a tempo / time-signature marker exactly on a bar line, with a small dialog.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finds Chorale wherever it is in the project and flips it on/off. Bind this to a footswitch and you have hands-free harmony.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Trim the LEFT edge of the selected items to the edit cursor - and if that would push the item over its neighbour on the same track, trim the neighbour's right edge to the cursor too. Items butt, they never overlap, so no surprise crossfades. Replaces "Item edit: Trim left edge of item to edit cursor" (Jason's Z key).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Trim the RIGHT edge of the selected items to the edit cursor - and if that would push the item over its neighbour on the same track, move the neighbour's left edge to the cursor too (its audio stays in place). Items butt, they never overlap. Replaces "Item edit: Trim right edge of item to edit cursor" (Jason's X key).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One button that flips the grid you are already on into triplets, and back. On 1/8? Press it and you are on 1/8 triplets. Press it again and you are back on 1/8. It works from every division, so there is no need for a separate 1/4T, 1/8T and 1/24 button cluttering the bar.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Wherever two items on the same track overlap, the EARLIER item is cut at the start of the later one, so items butt instead of sitting on top of each other - and the tangled crossfade at that join (a long fade-out on the earlier item / long fade-in on the later one) is reset to REAPER's short default fade. Fades elsewhere (a song-ending fade-out, say) are left alone. Works on the selected items; with none, on the selected tracks; with no selection at all, on every track. One undo step.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Clear every solo, arm, selection and the time selection in one press. The reset key.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Held-note tracking, an input gate and a note-off/CC64 flush, for clean live patch switching. Used by StageRig, but useful on its own anywhere a MIDI stream needs to be stopped without leaving notes hanging.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Sits at the top of a patch track FX chain and decides whether new notes reach the instrument below. Never blocks note-offs; flushes held notes + CC64 on close. Superseded by Note Tracker & Input Gate for StageRig, kept for simple rigs.\par}
}

